{"type": "survey_submit", "survey_type": "post", "puzzle_idx": null, "answers": {"puzzle_1_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_1_guesses": "never", "puzzle_2_rate_again": 3, "puzzle_2_guesses": "never", "puzzle_3_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_3_guesses": "never", "puzzle_1_rating_reason": "The biggest thing that determined the difficulty was how many rows could be solved from a glance (i.e rows where number of shaded cells and spacer rows added to ten by inspection). This nonogram, relative to other nonograms I have solved, had relatively few rows that could be filled by inspection making it difficult.", "puzzle_2_rating_reason": "Similar to the first, there were few rows that could be solved by inspection; however, I found it easier as my mind had warmed up to complete such hard nonograms after doing the first one.", "puzzle_3_rating_reason": "Once again, few rows solvable by inspection made this nonogram difficult. The difficulty spiked in comparison to number 2 because I began to get somewhat fatigued from puzzle solving.", "strategy": "The first strategy was to solve as many rows and columns by inspection. For example, if the numbers were 5 and 4, I could immediately eliminate that line from consideration and focus on harder sections.\n\nThe second strategy I utilized was to fill out a few rows in an almost nondeterministic fashion. For example, in rows where there was one less tile than needed to solve by inspection (i.e 4, 4) I would fill that line in such a way that all possible permutations of shaded tile that fit that pattern would be derivable. With these extra cells, I would then complete other lines. Finally, when I had more information, I would loop back to the nondeterministic line and complete it.", "comments": "I will say there was a bit of initial shock from the spike in difficulty between the warmups and first nonogram. I think if someone had less experience doing nonograms they likely would have taken considerably longer."}, "t": "2026-03-02T20:45:40.664931+00:00"}
